Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Executive","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-18","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-25","committee":"Executive","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-02","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-08-12","committee":"Executive","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Executive","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Burnley received \"Reasonable Assurance\" audit opinion for 2025/26 but Charter Walk acquisition had comprehensive controls","lead":"Burnley's internal audit concluded that the council has a good system of controls but minor weaknesses in design or consistency of application may put the achievement of some objectives at risk. The council received a \"Reasonable Assurance\" overall audit opinion for 2025/26, the second-highest rating available. However, when auditors reviewed the council's acquisition of Charter Walk shopping centre in Burnley town centre \u2014 a decision presenting wide-ranging commercial risks \u2014 they found the risks were properly identified, controlled and continue to be monitored, and members were fully briefed before the decision. Internal audit gave comprehensive assurance that controls were sound and consistently applied, with no remedial actions required. The council implemented 100 per cent of high-priority audit recommendations against its 100 per cent target, though some lower-priority recommendations have been delayed.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51999/51999.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Burnley audit finds good controls but minor weaknesses in 2025/26","lead":"Burnley council's internal audit for 2025/26 found that the authority has a good system of controls but minor weaknesses in design or consistency of application may put achievement of some objectives at risk. The council met 100% of its high-priority audit actions against its target. An audit review of the council's acquisition of the Charter Walk shopping centre found risks were identified, controlled and continue to be monitored, receiving comprehensive assurance. The council's chief internal auditor lacks required professional qualifications, which the council is working to address. Some lower-priority audit recommendations have been delayed in implementation.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51999/51999.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Burnley drew \u00a34.979m from reserves in Q1 2026/27 while identifying only \u00a383k of \u00a3259k budgeted savings","lead":"Burnley council drew \u00a34.979m from its reserves in the first quarter of 2026/27, reducing its earmarked reserves from \u00a324.339m to \u00a319.360m in three months. The council is drawing on its money set aside for known future needs because it is funding capital schemes\u2014buildings, roads and equipment\u2014through internal borrowing, using its own cash instead of taking bank loans to save on interest. However, the papers show this approach's sustainability \"remains under continual review\". At the same time, the council has identified only \u00a383k of the \u00a3259k in budgeted savings it committed to deliver this year, leaving \u00a3176k unaccounted for. The council's own papers state that current economic pressures \"may threaten the achievement of these targets\". If those savings are not found, the council's forecast break-even position will not materialise, and reserves will deplete faster still.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Revenue Monitoring Q1 2627 Revenue Report","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51968/51968.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Treasury Mgt Q1 Appendix 1 - Pru Ind working papers Q1 Final","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52132/52132.pdf","headline":"Financing costs forecast to reach 24\u201328% of net revenue stream, nearly double the 2025/26 level of 14.7%.","detail":"The ratio of financing costs to net revenue stream is forecast in the 24\u201328% range for the coming period, nearly double the 14.7% recorded in 2025/26. This sharp increase reflects both the elevated capital investment and the impact of higher interest rates on debt service. The council notes that net revenue stream assumptions for future years are based on 2026/27 levels and will be revised as part of MTFS updates, suggesting the ratio may move as the revenue base is reassessed.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Financing costs as a percentage of revenue is a core affordability indicator. A near-doubling in a single year indicates debt service is becoming a significantly larger claim on the council's revenue budget, potentially constraining flexibility for service delivery and signalling the need for careful debt management going forward.","figures":"Ratio of financing costs to net revenue stream: 14.7% (2025/26 actual) to 24.4%\u201328.6% (2026/27 forecast period), moderating to 24.1%\u201325.0% in later years","quote":"Ratio of financing costs to net revenue stream* 14.7% 28.6% 24.4% 24.2% 25.0% 24.1%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Treasury Mgt Q1 Appendix 1 - Pru Ind working papers Q1 Final","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52132/52132.pdf","headline":"Capital expenditure spiked to \u00a331 million for current year 2026/27 (revised estimate), 82% above prior year and well above the original forecast.","detail":"Burnley's capital expenditure for 2026/27 has been revised upward to \u00a331,025k, compared to \u00a317,047k actually spent in 2025/26 and the \u00a324,332k originally estimated for the current year. The revision represents an 82% increase from the prior year and a 27% upward revision from the original plan. Capital expenditure is then forecast to decline sharply to \u00a313,605k in 2027/28 and continue declining thereafter, suggesting this is a one-year peak in a planned, but frontloaded, capital programme.","category":"capital","matters":"A significant one-year spike in capital expenditure signals either new projects approved, existing schemes accelerated, or cost escalation brought forward into the current year. Lenders assessing borrowing requirements need to understand what is driving this concentration and when expenditure normalises; peer councils monitor whether this reflects genuine demand or budget management.","figures":"Capital Expenditure: \u00a317,047k (2025/26 actual), \u00a331,025k (2026/27 revised estimate), \u00a313,605k (2027/28), declining to \u00a310,077k by 2029/30","quote":"Capital Expenditure 17,047 24,332 31,025 13,605 12,804 10,077","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Treasury Mgt Q1 Report 2026-27 Final","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52131/52131.pdf","headline":"Council forecasts net treasury management saving of \u00a3120k for 2026-27, indicating positive investment and debt management performance","detail":"Against a full-year budget of \u00a3475k for interest and dividend income, Q1 returns were \u00a366k. The council is now forecasting a net treasury saving of \u00a3120k for the year, suggesting investment returns and borrowing cost management are outperforming budget expectations.","category":"income","matters":"A positive treasury variance demonstrates effective debt portfolio and investment management. In a period of economic weakness and inflationary pressure, a forecast saving shows the council is capturing value from short-term investments (achieving 3.60% average return) and managing borrowing costs efficiently.","figures":"Interest receivable Q1: \u00a366k; full-year budget for interest and dividends: \u00a3475k; forecast net saving: \u00a3120k. Average investment return: 3.60%","quote":"The current forecast for the year is a net Treasury Management saving of (\u00a3120k).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Treasury Mgt Q1 Report 2026-27 Final","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52131/52131.pdf","headline":"Council took no new borrowing in Q1 despite \u00a36.2m capital budget increase and repaid \u00a3234k of existing debt","detail":"The council did not enter any temporary market loans for cash flow purposes during April\u2013June. External debt fell by \u00a3234k from \u00a362.9m to \u00a362.7m through a scheduled annuity repayment on PWLB loans. This means the entire \u00a36.2m capital budget revision was financed from grants, revenue, and existing cash reserves without new borrowing.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Taking no new debt despite a major upward revision in capital spending demonstrates strong cash management and independent financing. It reduces reliance on debt markets at a time of elevated gilt yields (10-year yields reached 5.18% in May) and shows the council can fund growth from its own resources or grant funding.","figures":"External debt 1 April 2026: \u00a362,904k; 30 June 2026: \u00a362,670k; Change: \u2013\u00a3234k. PWLB debt: \u00a362,890k to \u00a362,656k","quote":"PWLB Loans \u2013 No maturity loan repayments were made during the period 1 April to 30 June 2026. One scheduled annuity repayment was made during the period totaling \u00a3234k.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-30","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Treasury Mgt Q1 Report 2026-27 Final","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52131/52131.pdf","headline":"Capital financing requirement revised down \u00a32.3m to \u00a388.1m despite higher capital spending, showing improved cash position","detail":"The CFR (underlying borrowing need for capital) was estimated at \u00a390.4m in the Treasury Management Strategy approved in March but revised down to \u00a388.1m at Q1. This reduction of 2.5% occurs despite the capital budget rising by \u00a36.2m, indicating the council's actual cash requirements are lower than the March forecast or that financing is more efficient.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A downward revision in CFR shows the council has better cash access or is using alternative financing more efficiently than planned. This reduces long-term debt need and borrowing costs, improving financial flexibility and debt serviceability\u2014key metrics lenders monitor.","figures":"CFR estimate March 2026: \u00a390.391m; CFR estimate Q1 2026: \u00a388.114m; reduction: \u00a32.277m","quote":"The Council's capital financing requirement (CFR) for 2026/27 was estimated at \u00a390.391m and approved as part of the TMSS in March 2026. At Q1, based on approved capital slippage from 2025/26, the Council's draft Statement of Accounts for 25/26 and in year capital budget adjustments, this is now estimated at \u00a388.114m.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Burnley","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-28","since_iso":"2026-08-29","distress":false,"headline":"Burnley received a 'Reasonable Assurance' overall audit opinion for 2025/26, indicating minor control weaknesses exist across the authority","lead":"The overall audit opinion directly reflects the robustness of the council's control environment, which is material to assessing financial and governance risk","also":["118 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny Committee on 2026-10-14","n_findings":119,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27 Appendix 1","top_paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52000/52000.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":205,"news_drafts_total":3}}